"If an EKS node becomes unhealthy, I follow a layer-by-layer approach: Kubernetes, node OS, and AWS infrastructure."

1. Verify the node status

kubectl get nodes

kubectl describe node <node-name>

I check whether the node is NotReady, under MemoryPressure, DiskPressure, or has network-related issues.

2. Check system pods

kubectl get pods -n kube-system

I verify critical components like:

aws-node (VPC CNI)
kube-proxy
CoreDNS

Failures in these components can impact node health.

3. Investigate the EC2 instance

I connect to the underlying EC2 instance and check:

df -h

free -h

top

Looking for:

Disk full
Memory exhaustion
CPU saturation
4. Check kubelet health

sudo systemctl status kubelet

sudo journalctl -u kubelet

If kubelet is down or unable to communicate with the control plane, the node becomes NotReady.

5. Validate networking

For EKS, I check:

Security groups
Route tables
Available subnet IPs
AWS VPC CNI logs

A common issue is subnet IP exhaustion, preventing pods from getting IP addresses.

6. Check AWS health

In AWS Console I verify:

EC2 instance status checks
Auto Scaling Group status
EKS node group health
7. Remediation

Depending on the root cause:

Restart kubelet
Clean disk space
Increase node capacity
Fix networking issues
Drain and replace the node

kubectl drain <node-name> --ignore-daemonsets

If the node is irrecoverable, I terminate the EC2 instance and let the managed node group automatically provision a replacement.

Interview-ready answer (1 minute)

"When an EKS node becomes unhealthy, I first check the node state using kubectl get nodes and kubectl describe node. Then I verify kube-system pods such as aws-node, kube-proxy, and CoreDNS. Next, I log into the EC2 instance to check CPU, memory, disk usage, and kubelet logs. I also validate AWS networking, subnet IP availability, and EC2 health checks. Once the root cause is identified, I either restart services, resolve resource constraints, fix networking issues, or drain and replace the node through the EKS managed node group. My goal is to restore workload availability quickly while identifying the underlying cause to prevent recurrence."

